     caption :: 'Monitor the desired outcome (absence of ischemic events)' ;
     description :: 'State Achievement monitoring of the shared achieve goal of the antithrombotic drugs: fewer ischemic events and MACE.' ;
     component :: 'Monitor_Ischemic_Events' ;
          task_definition :: 'Monitor_Ischemic_Events' ;
          ltwh :: 40,40,0,0;
end plan.

action  :: 'Display_Potential_ADEs' ;
     caption :: '# *Monitoring for ADEs:*  
1. Bleeding  
2. Drug interactions  
3. Spinal or epidural hematoma  
4. Perioperative bleeding and thrombosis  
5. Renal function (anticoagulant dosing)  
6. Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura  
7. Reduced clopidogrel effect (CYP2C19 poor metabolizer)  
8. Antiplatelet hypersensitivity  
9. Dyspnea (ticagrelor)  
10. Bradyarrhythmia (ticagrelor)  
11. Central sleep apnea (ticagrelor)  
12. C. difficile-associated diarrhea (PPI)  
13. Hypomagnesemia (PPI)  
14. Bone fracture (PPI)  
15. Acute interstitial nephritis (PPI)  
16. Cutaneous lupus erythematosus (PPI)  
17. Severe cutaneous reactions (PPI)  ' ;
     description :: 'Lists the adverse drug effects monitored in Monitor_Undesired_State; each is assessed by the Enquiry of the same name.' ;
     metaprops :: 'goal.noun_phrase=display of the potential adverse drug effects monitored for this therapy
goal.verb=order
role=ade_display
' ;
end action.

enquiry  :: 'Monitor_Bleeding' ;
     caption :: 'Monitor for bleeding (symptom review, Hb/Hct on suspicion)' ;
     description :: 'Monitors bleeding, including gastrointestinal and intracranial bleeding, as an adverse effect of Prescribe_Aspirin, Prescribe_Clopidogrel, Prescribe_Ticagrelor, Prescribe_Prasugrel, Prescribe_Vorapaxar, Prescribe_Low_Dose_Rivaroxaban and Prescribe_DOAC (and the avoid goal of Prescribe_PPI). The guideline names GI bleeding as a common side effect of DAPT, every antithrombotic label warns of serious bleeding and tells clinicians to promptly evaluate signs of blood loss, the P2Y12 labels say to manage bleeding without stopping when possible, and the DOAC labels say their effect cannot be monitored with standard coagulation tests. Order: bleeding symptom review with hemoglobin and hematocrit on suspected blood loss, each follow-up visit, with same-day evaluation of bleeding symptoms or unexplained hypotension. Patient: report any unusual, prolonged or excessive bleeding or bruising, blood in the urine, red or black tarry stools, vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, coughing up blood, fainting or a sudden severe headache, and seek emergency care for heavy bleeding. Clinician: promptly evaluate signs of blood loss with hemoglobin and hematocrit, suspect bleeding in any hypotensive patient after angiography, PCI, CABG or surgery, stop an anticoagulant for active pathological bleeding, manage antiplatelet-related bleeding without stopping the antiplatelet when possible, and do not use PT, INR, aPTT or anti-factor Xa levels to monitor a DOAC.' ;
     metaprops :: 'ServiceRequest.code=bleeding symptom review with hemoglobin and hematocrit on suspected blood loss
ServiceRequest.intent=order
ServiceRequest.occurrenceTiming=each follow-up visit, with same-day evaluation of bleeding symptoms or unexplained hypotension
ServiceRequest.patientInstruction=report any unusual, prolonged or excessive bleeding or bruising, blood in the urine, red or black tarry stools, vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, coughing up blood, fainting or a sudden severe headache, and seek emergency care for heavy bleeding
ServiceRequest.performerInstruction=promptly evaluate signs of blood loss with hemoglobin and hematocrit, suspect bleeding in any hypotensive patient after angiography, PCI, CABG or surgery, stop an anticoagulant for active pathological bleeding, manage antiplatelet-related bleeding without stopping the antiplatelet when possible, and do not use PT, INR, aPTT or anti-factor Xa levels to monitor a DOAC
applies_when=all_patients_on_therapy
assistant_invention=the symptom question at each scheduled visit (the labels state prompt evaluation of signs but no visit cadence) and hemoglobin and hematocrit as the triggered test for the antiplatelets
category=undesired_ade
cq=CQ3, CQ5, CQ8
goal.noun_phrase=ServiceRequest.intent \\= order; ServiceRequest.code \\= bleeding symptom review with hemoglobin and hematocrit on suspected blood loss; ServiceRequest.occurrenceTiming \\= each follow-up visit, with same-day evaluation of bleeding symptoms or unexplained hypotension; ServiceRequest.patientInstruction \\= report any unusual, prolonged or excessive bleeding or bruising, blood in the urine, red or black tarry stools, vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, coughing up blood, fainting or a sudden severe headache, and seek emergency care for heavy bleeding; ServiceRequest.performerInstruction \\= promptly evaluate signs of blood loss with hemoglobin and hematocrit, suspect bleeding in any hypotensive patient after angiography, PCI, CABG or surgery, stop an anticoagulant for active pathological bleeding, manage antiplatelet-related bleeding without stopping the antiplatelet when possible, and do not use PT, INR, aPTT or anti-factor Xa levels to monitor a DOAC
goal.verb=order
guideline_status=not_specified_as_a_guideline_monitoring_task
instrument=patient symptom report with hemoglobin and hematocrit on suspicion
knowledge_origin=guideline text 16 and every antithrombotic label\'s bleeding warning
literature_basis=the guideline names GI bleeding as a common side effect of DAPT, every antithrombotic label warns of serious bleeding and tells clinicians to promptly evaluate signs of blood loss, the P2Y12 labels say to manage bleeding without stopping when possible, and the DOAC labels say their effect cannot be monitored with standard coagulation tests
monitored_target=bleeding, including gastrointestinal and intracranial bleeding, as an adverse effect of Prescribe_Aspirin, Prescribe_Clopidogrel, Prescribe_Ticagrelor, Prescribe_Prasugrel, Prescribe_Vorapaxar, Prescribe_Low_Dose_Rivaroxaban and Prescribe_DOAC (and the avoid goal of Prescribe_PPI)
monitoring_frequency_basis=symptom review at every visit because bleeding is the most frequent serious ADE of every drug in the CIG, with event-driven testing as the labels direct
monitoring_stance=recommended
ontology.coding=SNOMED\\:131148009
ontology.label=Bleeding (finding)
provenance_type=package_insert_specified
quote.1=p.e55, text 16\\: "Increased bleeding including gastrointestinal bleeding is a common side effect of DAPT"
quote.2=\\u00A75.2\\: "Promptly evaluate any signs or symptoms of blood loss and consider the need for blood replacement."
quote.3=\\u00A75.1\\: "Suspect bleeding in any patient who is hypotensive and has recently undergone coronary angiography, PCI, CABG, or other surgical procedures"
quote.4=Boxed Warning\\: "If possible, manage bleeding without discontinuing BRILINTA."
quote.5=\\u00A75.3\\: "The anticoagulant effect of SAVAYSA cannot be reliably monitored with standard laboratory testing."
quote.6=Stop use\\: "signs of stomach bleeding\\: feel faint vomit blood have bloody or black stool have stomach pain that does not get better"
quote.7=Boxed Warning\\: "Antiplatelet agents, including ZONTIVITY, increase the risk of bleeding, including ICH and fatal bleeding"
quote.8=\\u00A75.2\\: "P2Y12 inhibitors (thienopyridines), including Plavix, increase the risk of bleeding."
source.1=2023 AHA/ACC CCD Guideline (Virani et al., DOI 10.1161/CIR.0000000000001168), \\u00A74.3.1 supportive text 16 (p.e55)
source.2=XARELTO (rivaroxaban) US Prescribing Information 2026, \\u00A75.2 Risk of Bleeding
source.3=EFFIENT (prasugrel) US Prescribing Information 2022, \\u00A75.1 General Risk of Bleeding
source.4=BRILINTA (ticagrelor) US Prescribing Information 2026, Boxed Warning
